**Assert the empty-order error**

No assertion checks the required error for an empty order. Add a unit test that checks the message:
```js
test('rejects orders with no items', () => {
  assert.throws(() => applyDiscount({ items: [] }), { message: 'empty order' });
});
```

<details><summary>Why this was flagged</summary>

kind: missing_test, confidence: high
</details>

<!-- specguard:fp=e1a7fcd2b144e486732fc89e5963542bd776cb7a -->

**Test the discount threshold boundary**

No assertion checks the 100 threshold. Add unit tests for the value at the threshold and just below it:
```js
test('subtracts five at a total of 100', () => {
  assert.equal(applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 100, qty: 1 }] }), 95);
});

test('does not subtract five below 100', () => {
  assert.equal(applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 99, qty: 1 }] }), 99);
});
```

<details><summary>Why this was flagged</summary>

kind: missing_test, confidence: high
</details>

<!-- specguard:fp=7e9ea60c544fa00ebdd2b592034339bf865804d5 -->

**Test credit deduction and the zero floor**

No assertion checks credit deduction or the zero floor. Add unit tests for both outcomes:
```js
test('subtracts store credit from the total', () => {
  assert.equal(applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 50, qty: 1 }], credit: 20 }), 30);
});

test('floors the total at zero when credit exceeds it', () => {
  assert.equal(applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 10, qty: 1 }], credit: 20 }), 0);
});
```

<details><summary>Why this was flagged</summary>

kind: missing_test, confidence: high
</details>

